= Earl Grey (伯爵茶)

_A *flavoured black tea*: a fully-oxidized *Camellia sinensis* base (classically a China black of the Keemun 祁門 type, now commonly Ceylon, Assam or a blend) carrying the cold-pressed peel oil of the *bergamot orange*, *Citrus bergamia* Risso & Poit. The substance is a *19th-century British commercial blend*, and this entry treats it as such. Two scope points govern everything below. First, *it is a flavoured tea, not a scented tea in the Chinese technical sense*: the aromatic is an extracted oil applied to finished leaf, not volatiles adsorbed from living flowers over repeated cycles, so Earl Grey has *no place in the Chinese 花茶 category* that jasmine tea occupies — compare jasmine-tea.typ, which is the structural counterpart done the other way. Second, *neither component is classical anywhere*: the tea base postdates the Bencao Gangmu (see red-tea.typ), and *Citrus bergamia is itself a late Calabrian hybrid with no attestation in any classical pharmacopoeia* — Chinese, Ayurvedic or Greco-Arabic. Every traditional-system section here is therefore *reasoned analysis, not classical*, and doubly so; the entry says so at each point rather than once. Status: British — fully traditional since the 1880s and the single most recognizable flavoured tea in the world. 中醫, Ayurveda, Tibb, Greco-Roman: not classical._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*English:* *_Earl Grey_* — named for *Charles Grey, 2nd Earl Grey* (1764–1845), Whig Prime Minister 1830–34, under whom the Reform Act 1832 and the Slavery Abolition Act 1833 passed. *The name describes a man, not the tea*, which is worth saying because readers reasonably assume the colour-word is descriptive: the surname *Grey* is an Anglo-Norman byname and has nothing to do with the liquor's colour, which is in any case amber-red.

*On the origin story, which is folklore and should be labelled as such.* The familiar account — that a Chinese mandarin presented the recipe to Grey in gratitude for a son rescued from drowning — is *apocryphal*. Grey never went to China; bergamot is a Mediterranean fruit with no Chinese tea tradition; and *the name is not attested in print until the 1880s*, some four decades after Grey's death. The earliest known commercial references are English advertisements of the 1880s for "Grey's Mixture" and "Earl Grey's Mixture," and the competing house claims (Twinings, Jacksons of Piccadilly) to original possession of the recipe are *20th-century marketing narratives*, not documented 1830s transactions. *A second, more plausible and also unproven account* holds that bergamot was applied to *mask inferior or stale leaf*, or to imitate the natural citrus-floral note of fine Chinese tea — the adulteration hypothesis, which fits the well-documented 19th-century British tea-adulteration record but has no direct evidence tying it to this blend. *Both accounts are flagged as contested; neither should be repeated as established.*#footnote[The apocryphal gift-story, the 1880s first attestation, and the competing house claims are treated in the tea-history literature: Mair & Hoh, _The True History of Tea_ (Thames & Hudson, 2009); Pettigrew, _A Social History of Tea_. The _OED_ entry for "Earl Grey" records the late-19th-c. attestation date. The adulteration hypothesis is widely repeated and is here flagged as plausible-but-undemonstrated; 19th-c. British tea adulteration generally is well documented (Burnett, _Plenty and Want_). Charles Grey's dates and ministry: standard British political history.]

*Latin:* *_Citrus bergamia_ Risso & Poit.*, family *Rutaceae* — the bergamot orange. Pharmacy name *_Bergamottae aetheroleum_* / *_Oleum bergamottae_* (bergamot essential oil), the cold-pressed oil of the *flavedo* (outer coloured peel). *Its origin is unresolved and its date is late:* it is a hybrid, most genomic work pointing to *bitter orange (_C. × aurantium_) × citron or lemon*, and it appears in Calabria probably in the 17th century. *This is why it has no classical pharmacology:* it did not exist when any of the canons were written. The base tea is *_Camellia sinensis_* — see green-tea.typ for the full Camellia framework and red-tea.typ for the oxidation category.

*Bergamot, etymology — contested, and the two accounts are not equally good.* English _bergamot_ ← French _bergamote_ ← Italian _bergamotta_. The traditional derivation is from the Lombard city of *Bergamo*, where the oil was supposedly first traded; *the objection is geographical* — Bergamo is in the far north and the fruit is grown 1,000 km away in Calabria, with no documented Bergamasque trade in it. The competing derivation, which lexicography generally prefers, is from *Turkish _beg armudu_ / _bey armudu_*, "lord's pear" or "prince's pear" — originally a pear variety, the name transferred to the citrus on a shape-or-scent resemblance. *The OED and Italian lexicography treat the Turkish route as the likelier source of the word and the Bergamo account as folk etymology*, though the transfer from pear to citrus is itself not fully documented. Flagged accordingly.#footnote[_OED_, s.v. "bergamot" (distinguishing the pear sense from the citrus sense and recording the _beg armudu_ derivation); standard Italian etymological lexicography (Battisti-Alessio; DELI) on _bergamotta_. The Bergamo derivation is the popular account and is treated here as folk etymology, flagged per this project's convention. _Citrus bergamia_ hybrid parentage: modern citrus genomics — the bitter-orange × citron/lemon reconstruction is the current consensus but should be verified against current phylogenetic literature, which has revised citrus relationships repeatedly.]

*Two name-traps, both live in the herbal literature.*
- *"Bergamot" the mint, not the citrus.* *_Monarda didyma_* (scarlet beebalm) and *_M. fistulosa_* ("wild bergamot"), family *Lamiaceae*, are North American mints called "bergamot" purely for a similar scent. *_M. didyma_* is the *Oswego tea* of the Iroquois and of colonial America, drunk as a tea substitute during the pre-Revolutionary boycotts. *It is botanically unrelated to _Citrus bergamia_, has an entirely different chemistry, and has nothing to do with Earl Grey* — a confusion that recurs in English-language herbal writing, where "bergamot tea" may mean either thing.
- *"Bergamot" rendered as 佛手柑 in Chinese.* Chinese-language and some Western sources translate bergamot as *佛手柑 (fóshǒugān)*, which *properly denotes a different drug*: *佛手 (fóshǒu,* Buddha's-hand citron, *_Citrus medica_ var. _sarcodactylis_*, pharmacy name *Citri Sarcodactylis Fructus*), a *canonical Chinese medicinal* — acrid, bitter and sour, warm; entering Liver, Spleen, Stomach and Lung; *疏肝理氣*, *和胃止痛*, *燥濕化痰*. *The conflation is consequential*, because 佛手 has a real Chinese-medical standing that bergamot does not, and a reader meeting "bergamot" in a translated Chinese source may be reading about 佛手 instead. Modern Chinese renders the bergamot orange proper as *香檸檬 (xiāngníngméng,* "fragrant lemon") or by phonetic loan; *usage is unsettled and should be verified against the specific source* rather than assumed.

*Chinese:* *伯爵茶 (bójuéchá,* "earl tea") — the standard modern Chinese name for the blend, a *calque* on the English title; also *格雷伯爵茶 (Géléi bójuéchá)*, adding a phonetic transcription of "Grey." *There is no classical Chinese name*, the substance being a Western import; it is sold and understood in China as a foreign beverage rather than as a tea category.

*Sanskrit:* *No entry, classical or modern-technical.* Tea reached South Asia as a crop only in the 19th century (see red-tea.typ) and bergamot is not cultivated there; the blend circulates in India under its English name. The Hindi for the base is *काली चाय (kālī chāy,* black tea), per red-tea.typ.

*Arabic:* *شاي إيرل جراي (shāy īrl grāy)* — phonetic. The fruit: *البرغموت (al-barghamūt)*, a modern European loan. *No classical Arabic name exists*, for the same reason as above.

== Processing and category definition

*Earl Grey is made by flavouring, not by scenting, and the distinction is technical rather than pedantic.* Set the two processes side by side:

- *Jasmine tea (花茶)* — finished tea is *layered with living flowers* over repeated cycles (*窨花*), absorbing volatiles as the buds open; the spent flowers are sieved out and the cycle repeated two to five times or more. The aroma enters by *adsorption and redistribution into the leaf matrix*. See jasmine-tea.typ.
- *Earl Grey* — an *extracted oil or flavour preparation is applied directly* to finished black tea, by spraying or tumbling, sometimes with dried citrus peel added as a visible garnish. One step, no cycles, no flowers.

*The consequence is categorical:* the Chinese six-category system (*六大茶類*) classifies by processing, and its adjacent *花茶* category is defined by the scenting process. *Earl Grey satisfies neither*, and there is no Chinese category that accommodates it — it is a Western artifact of a Western industry, and the honest statement is that the Chinese taxonomy simply has no slot for it rather than that it belongs in 花茶 by analogy.

*Three quality tiers, which differ pharmacologically and not merely in flavour:*
1. *Cold-pressed natural bergamot oil*, typically Calabrian. Carries the full volatile spectrum *and* the non-volatile furanocoumarin fraction (bergapten, bergamottin, citropten).
2. *FCF — "furocoumarin-free" bergamot oil*, from which the furanocoumarins have been stripped. Now standard in many food and cosmetic applications. *Retains the aroma and removes the phototoxic and CYP3A4-inhibiting fraction.*
3. *Nature-identical or synthetic flavouring* — reconstructed blends built chiefly on *linalyl acetate* and *linalool*. *This is most of the market*, and it contains *no furanocoumarins at all*.

*Which tier a given product uses is usually not stated on the label*, and the distinction is the single most important practical fact in this entry: nearly everything said below about furanocoumarin pharmacology applies to tier 1 and *not* to tiers 2 and 3.

*The base.* Classically a China black, with *祁門 (Keemun)* the usual reference — the same Anhui tea that red-tea.typ identifies as the classical English Breakfast benchmark. Modern commercial Earl Grey commonly uses Ceylon, Assam or blended material, frequently var. *assamica*, which is maltier and more robust and stands up to the oil differently than the lighter Keemun.#footnote[The flavouring-versus-scenting distinction and the spray/tumble application method are standard tea-industry practice: Heiss & Heiss, _The Story of Tea_ (Ten Speed Press, 2007); Hicks, _The Production of Commercial Tea_. FCF bergamot oil and its regulatory adoption in food and cosmetics: IFRA standards and food-flavouring regulatory literature — verify against current standards, which have been revised. The observation that the Chinese six-category system has no slot for a directly-flavoured tea is this book's framing, following from the system's process-based definition.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Reasoned analysis, not classical — and doubly so. Earl Grey has no Chinese-medical entry of any kind. Its base category 紅茶 is itself post-_Bencao Gangmu_ (1578) and placed by Qing-era and modern tea-dietetics rather than by the early Bencao (see red-tea.typ); and_ Citrus bergamia _has no Chinese drug entry at all, the species being unknown to the Chinese pharmacopoeia. The placement below reasons from the red-tea entry for the base and from the canonical aromatic citrus-peel drugs — 陳皮 (see tangerine-peel.typ) and 佛手 — for the addition. It is an extension on the classical axes, not an attestation._

*Property (性 / 氣):* *Warm (溫) — and more decisively warm than the plain base.* This is the entry's central structural point, and it is best seen by contrast with jasmine tea. *Jasmine tea takes a cool base and adds a warm aromatic, so the compound moves toward neutral (平)* — the scenting moderates. *Earl Grey takes an already-warm base and adds a warm, acrid, drying aromatic, so nothing is moderated and the compound ends warmer and drier than either tea it is built from.* Of the teas treated in this book, Earl Grey would sit at the warm extreme alongside plain 紅茶 and the post-fermented dark teas.

*Flavor (味):* *Sweet (甘)* and malty from the fully-oxidized base; *acrid/pungent (辛)* and *aromatic (芳香)* from the peel oil, with a faint *sour (酸)* and *bitter (苦)* citrus edge. The acrid-aromatic is the diagnostic addition.

*Channels entered (歸經):* the red-tea channels — *Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Heart (心)*, with some readings adding Lung (肺) and Kidney (腎) — *plus the citrus-peel channels*, which contribute what the base lacks: *Liver (肝)*. Reasoned from 陳皮 and 佛手, both of which enter Liver, Spleen, Stomach and Lung. *The Liver entry is the whole pharmacological interest of the addition*, since it is what makes the compound a qi-coursing drink and not merely a warming one.

*Actions & indications (reasoned):*
- *From the base* (see red-tea.typ): *暖胃 / 溫中* (warms the Stomach and the middle), *助消化* (aids digestion, especially of fat-and-meat-heavy meals), *提神* (refreshes the spirit), *利尿* (mild diuretic).
- *From the bergamot peel oil*, reasoned on the 陳皮 / 佛手 axis: *理氣* (regulates qi), *疏肝解鬱* (courses the Liver and resolves constraint), *和胃* (harmonizes the Stomach), *醒神 / 開胃* (rouses the spirit, opens the appetite), and *化痰* (transforms phlegm) weakly — weakly because the quantity of oil in a cup is very small, which is a point the pharmacology section takes up.
- *Combined functional register:* a *warming digestive-and-mood* tea — the *暖胃 + 疏肝* pairing, taken after a heavy meal or in the mid-afternoon. *This register is a genuine cross-system convergence rather than an artifact of the reasoning*: the British practice of drinking it hot in the afternoon, the Chinese functional-dietetic use of 紅茶 after fatty meals, and the citrus-peel qi-moving tradition all land in the same place. See *辛溫香燥* in zz-glossary.typ for the property-class the addition belongs to.

*Cautions (reasoned):* *contraindicated in heat patterns, and more so than plain red tea* — blazing fire, hypertension with internal heat, peptic ulcer or reflux with heat. *Yin vacuity with dryness* is the characteristic mismatch: a warm base plus an acrid-drying aromatic in a patient whose deficit is fluid. Insomnia and yin-vacuity heat (the caffeine plus the warming aromatic). *The acrid-warm-aromatic-drying (辛溫香燥) caution class recorded in this book for 木香 applies by the same reasoning* — see six-gentlemen-decoction-with-aucklandia-and-amomum.typ. Pregnancy: caffeine load.#footnote[All attributions in this section are reasoned extension, not citation. The base is from red-tea.typ, whose own placement rests on Qing-era and modern Chinese tea-dietetic literature. The aromatic addition is reasoned from the canonical citrus-peel drugs 陳皮 and 佛手 — Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed., for both; _Chinese Pharmacopoeia_ for 佛手 (Citri Sarcodactylis Fructus). The warm-plus-warm versus cool-plus-warm contrast with jasmine tea is this book's framing of the saṃskāra logic the jasmine entry sets out.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical. No classical Sanskrit entry exists for Earl Grey, for the tea base, or for bergamot — the first is a British blend, the second entered South Asia in the 19th century, and the third is a European hybrid never cultivated there. The reading below follows red-tea.typ for the base and reasons the aromatic citrus addition onto the standard parameters._

*Reasoned rasa:* *Madhura* (मधुर, sweet — the malty base), *Tikta* (तिक्त, bitter — moderate, less than green tea), *Kaṣāya* (कषाय, astringent — the theaflavin-thearubigin profile, less astringent than catechins), plus *Kaṭu* (कटु, pungent) and a faint *Amla* (अम्ल, sour) *from the peel oil*. Five of the six rasas are represented, which is unusual and is simply what adding a citrus aromatic to a malty astringent base produces.

*Reasoned guṇa:* *Laghu* (लघु, light), *Snigdha* (स्निग्ध, slightly unctuous — the distinctive red-tea shift away from green tea's rūkṣa, per red-tea.typ), *Tīkṣṇa* (तीक्ष्ण, sharp — caffeine and the aromatic), *Sūkṣma* (सूक्ष्म, subtle and penetrating — the volatile-aromatic property, applied here as jasmine-tea.typ applies it to floral volatiles).

*Reasoned vīrya:* *Uṣṇa* (उष्ण, heating) — *and more decisively uṣṇa than plain red tea*, the aromatic reinforcing rather than moderating. This converges with the 中醫 warm reading above.

*Reasoned vipāka:* *An in-book tension, stated rather than smoothed.* red-tea.typ gives the base's vipāka as *Madhura* (मधुर), on the malty-caramel notes. The pungent-aromatic addition argues for a shift toward *Kaṭu* (कटु). *This entry gives kaṭu-leaning-madhura and flags the reading as interpretive*; the honest position is that vipāka assignment for a post-classical compound has no arbiter, and the two component readings pull in different directions. Worth noting rather than resolving by assertion.

*Prabhāva:* *None.* Per this project's prabhāva rule, the explicit statement is required: *no classical text assigns a prabhāva to this substance, because no classical text treats it* — and on reasoned grounds the actions follow predictably from kaṭu-uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa-sūkṣma plus the base's profile, with nothing left over. *One candidate deserves to be named and then declined:* the anxiolytic pharmacology of *linalool and linalyl acetate* is not obviously predictable from a pungent-heating profile, and could be dressed as a prabhāva-level action. *This entry declines that move.* Prabhāva is a category of the classical system, and retrofitting modern receptor pharmacology into it is a category error — it would be *inventing* a classical attribution for a substance the classics never saw, which is precisely what the rule exists to prevent. The linalool pharmacology is real and belongs in the modern section, where it is stated.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* *Pacifies Kapha* — the clearest indication; uṣṇa, kaṭu, laghu and tīkṣṇa all act against Kapha, and the aromatic addition strengthens this relative to plain red tea. *Pacifies Vāta* in moderation — the snigdha-uṣṇa-madhura base is Vāta-friendly (per red-tea.typ) and the aromatic is *vāta-anulomana*; but the tīkṣṇa-laghu caffeine load aggravates Vāta in excess, and the aromatic's drying edge cuts against the snigdha. *Aggravates Pitta, and more than plain red tea does* — the uṣṇa vīrya, the tīkṣṇa guṇa and the caffeine compound. *Net: a Kapha tea*, and the wrong tea for a Pitta constitution or a Pitta season.

*Reasoned karma:* *Dīpana* and *pācana* (digestive-kindling and digestive-ripening — the after-meal use, well-supported across traditions for the base), *vāta-anulomana* (the carminative aromatic register), *kaphahara*, *hṛdya* (cardiotonic), *medhya* (mild), *rucya / ruci-vardhana* (appetite-promoting, via the aromatic), *ucchvāsa-śuddhi* (breath-clearing), *manas-prasādana* (mind-gladdening — reasoned from the aromatic, and the cross-system convergence with the Chinese 疏肝解鬱 and the Tibb *mufarriḥ* below). In excess: *pitta-prakopa*, *amlapitta* (hyperacidity), *nidrā-nāśana* (sleep-destroying), *rasa-kṣaya* (depletion of the fluid tissue — the drying effect of a tīkṣṇa-rūkṣa-leaning aromatic on an astringent base).#footnote[Reasoned dravyaguna throughout; no classical citation is available or claimed. Base parameters follow red-tea.typ, whose reasoning in turn parallels P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, on kāphī (coffee) as a warming stimulant. The vipāka tension with red-tea.typ is stated rather than resolved. The prabhāva refusal is this book's application of its own prabhāva rule, which requires saying so when the canon assigns none.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) and (N): none, and the absence is categorical rather than an omission.* Neither the saṃhitās nor the nighaṇṭus treat tea, bergamot or this blend; there is no classical incompatibility literature to report. *Said plainly rather than padded with inferred entries.*
- *(F) Folk / regional:* the general tea rules, inherited from the base (see red-tea.typ and green-tea.typ) — not on an empty stomach, not late in the evening, not alongside iron-rich meals. The British convention of taking Earl Grey *without milk, or with a slice of lemon*, is a folk rule with a sensible basis: the citrus volatiles sit poorly against milk casein, and the lemon reinforces the existing aromatic-sour register.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* ~40–60 mg per cup, as for the red-tea base; cup-for-cup more than green tea, well under coffee. *This is the limiting component of the whole substance*, not the bergamot.
  - *Furanocoumarins — the real but heavily-qualified concern, and the qualification matters more than the concern.* Cold-pressed natural bergamot oil contains *bergapten (5-methoxypsoralen, 5-MOP)*, *bergamottin* and *citropten*. Two effects follow in principle: *phototoxicity* and *CYP3A4 inhibition* — the grapefruit-interaction mechanism, already recorded in this book for angelica-root.typ and in milder form for tangerine-peel.typ. *Four qualifications, all of which cut the same way:* (a) the oil load in flavoured tea is very small; (b) the furanocoumarins are the *non-volatile* fraction and are poorly extracted into a water infusion; (c) *FCF oil has them removed*; and (d) *most commercial Earl Grey is flavoured synthetically and contains none at all*. *The residual real-world case* is heavy daily consumption of naturally-flavoured Earl Grey by someone on a critical-dose CYP3A4 substrate — statins, calcium-channel blockers, immunosuppressants. Worth knowing; not worth alarm.
  - *Phototoxicity is a route-specific hazard and is not a drinking hazard.* *Berloque dermatitis* — the pigmented streaking from bergamot-containing perfume plus sunlight — arises from *topical* application, as does phytophotodermatitis generally (see angelica-root.typ for the mechanism). *Drinking Earl Grey does not cause it*, and sources that transfer the perfume caution to the beverage are making a route error.
  - *The "Earl Grey intoxication" case report, reported with its dose because the dose is the point.* A single *Lancet* correspondence (2002) described a patient consuming roughly *four litres a day* who developed muscle cramps, fasciculations, paraesthesia and blurred vision, resolving on cessation; bergapten's inhibition of potassium channels was proposed as the mechanism. *This is n = 1 at an extreme dose*, it has not been replicated, and it does not establish a hazard at ordinary consumption. *It is cited here because it is the only human report of bergamot toxicity from tea in the literature and is widely repeated without its dose.*
  - *Iron absorption:* reduced, as for all teas, but *less than green tea* — theaflavins and thearubigins bind iron less than catechins do (per red-tea.typ). Relevant in iron-deficiency anaemia; separate from iron-rich meals by 1–2 hours.
  - *Citrus allergy:* avoid.
  - *Pregnancy:* caffeine ≤200 mg/day; no specific bergamot concern at flavouring quantities.
  - *A sourcing-and-literature incompatibility, which belongs here because it is a de facto error rather than a pharmacological one:* the clinical literature on *bergamot polyphenolic fraction (BPF)* and blood lipids is *not* evidence about Earl Grey. See the modern section, where the point is made properly.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Not classical, and here the gap is wider than for most substances in this book. For jasmine tea the scenting flower is fully Avicennan even though the compound is not (see jasmine-tea.typ); for Earl Grey_ neither _component is. Tea reached the Persian-Arabic-Ottoman world only in the 17th–19th c. (see green-tea.typ), and_ Citrus bergamia _is a 17th-c. Calabrian hybrid that no medieval author could have described. What follows is reasoned placement on a canonical axis, clearly marked. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the humoral framework applied._

*Arabic:* شاي إيرل جراي (_shāy īrl grāy_); the fruit البرغموت (_al-barghamūt_) — both modern loans, with no classical name.

*The canonical axis the placement reasons from.* The *citrus peel class is fully Avicennan*: قشر الأترج (_qishr al-utrujj_, citron peel) and قشر النارنج (_qishr al-nāranj_, sour-orange peel), both treated in Ibn Sīnā's *al-Qānūn* II among the simple drugs and classified *Hot and Dry in the 1st–2nd degree*. *This is exactly the move tangerine-peel.typ makes* for the same reason — a post-Avicennan citrus placed on the canonical peel axis rather than left unplaced — and this entry follows that precedent rather than inventing a new one.

*Mizāj (reasoned):* *Hot and Dry, 1st–2nd degree* for the bergamot peel fraction, on the canonical citrus-peel classification; the fully-oxidized tea base reads *Hot and Dry in the 1st degree* by the same post-classical extension green-tea.typ and red-tea.typ describe. *The compound is therefore Hot-and-Dry throughout, with no cooling or moistening element anywhere in it* — a point that converges exactly with the 中醫 warm-and-drying reading and the Ayurvedic uṣṇa, and which is the cleanest cross-system agreement in this entry.

*Af'āl (reasoned actions):* *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent — the citrus-peel signature), *muqawwī al-miʿda* (stomach-strengthening), *kāsir al-rīḥ* (carminative, wind-breaking), *muqawwī al-qalb* (cardiotonic), *mufarriḥ* (exhilarant, gladdening — the canonical action of the aromatic citrus peels and of the citron, and the Tibb term for the register the Chinese call 疏肝解鬱 and Ayurveda *manas-prasādana*), *muḥallil* (resolvent) weakly.

*Istemālāt (reasoned indications):* *amrāḍ al-balgham* (phlegmatic diseases — the Hot-Dry drug against a Cold-Moist humour, the standard logic); weak digestion and post-prandial heaviness; low spirits and *ḍuʿf al-rūḥ*.

*Mawāne' / Iḥtirāzāt (reasoned contraindications):* *ṣafrāwī* (bilious) temperaments and all hot-dry conditions — the Pitta-equivalent caution, and the same mismatch the 中醫 and Ayurvedic sections identify; insomnia; dry constitutions.

*Muṣliḥ (reasoned corrective):* *milk, or a little sugar* — which is what British practice does anyway, and the convergence is worth marking: a Hot-Dry drug corrected by a Cool-Moist vehicle is textbook Tibb reasoning arrived at by taste rather than by theory. See milk.typ, honey.typ. *Flagged as reasoned, not as a recorded Unani pairing.*

*Badal (reasoned substitute):* *qishr al-nāranj* (sour-orange peel) on a black-tea base would occupy the same slot, being the canonical drug the bergamot placement borrows from.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī:* *No attestation, and none should be manufactured.* Neither tea nor bergamot appears in the Qur'ān or the hadith corpus. *The nearest thing is not an attestation of this substance:* the *utrujjah* (citron) appears in a well-known hadith likening the believer who reads the Qur'ān to that fruit for its fragrance and taste — but that is *a different species, used as a simile rather than as medicine*, and citing it here would be doubly misleading. Recorded only to close off the inference.#footnote[The canonical citrus-peel classification — _qishr al-utrujj_ and _qishr al-nāranj_, Hot and Dry 1st–2nd degree — is from Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_al-Adwiya al-Mufrada_); the same framework and the same post-classical-extension logic are used in `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`, which this entry follows. Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, for the broader citrus material. Everything applied to bergamot specifically is reasoned extension: the species postdates the entire classical Arabic corpus. The utrujjah simile hadith is in the canonical collections (Bukhārī, Muslim) and is a simile, not a pharmacological attestation; it is named here only to pre-empt its misuse.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*None, and the absence is structural rather than incidental.* Earl Grey has no place in the Chinese formulary, no 中醫 drug entry, and — per the processing section above — *no category in the 六大茶類 system or its 花茶 adjunct*. It circulates in China as 伯爵茶, an imported Western beverage.

*What occupies the functional slot in Chinese practice, marked as comparison and not as equivalence:* the aromatic citrus-peel qi-movers — *陳皮* (aged tangerine peel; see tangerine-peel.typ) and *佛手* (Buddha's-hand citron) — do in Chinese pharmacy what bergamot does in this blend, and *佛手茶* (sliced dried 佛手 infused) is a real Chinese preparation in the same register. *That is a parallel, not a lineage*: nothing connects the British blend to it.

=== Ayurveda

*No classical formulas.* Earl Grey is a 20th-century cosmopolitan import in India.

*The indigenous parallel, marked as comparison:* *masala chai* — black tea with milk and warming spices — occupies the same slot as Earl Grey, a warm aromatic layered on a fully-oxidized tea base, and red-tea.typ identifies it as the canonical Vāta-pacifying tea preparation. *The two reach the same constitutional destination by different routes*: masala chai by whole warming spices with a *snigdha* milk vehicle, Earl Grey by a single citrus volatile oil, conventionally without milk. *The masala chai route is the more thoroughly Vāta-pacifying of the two*, the milk and the fat supplying what the bergamot's drying aromatic does not.

=== Western / Greco-Roman

*No Greco-Roman tradition* — tea was unknown to the classical Mediterranean, and bergamot postdates antiquity by some fifteen centuries. *This section carries the substance's real tradition, which is British and modern.*

*The standard preparation:* 2–3 g per cup, water *at or near the boil* (unlike the cooler water green and jasmine teas require), *3–5 minute steep*. Taken *hot*. *Conventionally without milk*, or with a *slice of lemon* — the one major British black tea for which milk is not the default, the citrus volatiles sitting poorly against casein.

*Named derivatives and variants:*
- *Lady Grey* — a *Twinings trademark* (1990s): Earl Grey with added lemon and orange peel and *cornflower petals*; lighter and more overtly citrus.
- *Russian Earl Grey* — with added citrus peel and often lemongrass; the "Russian caravan" naming register.
- *French Earl Grey* — with added *rose* and often hibiscus petals.
- *Earl Grey Crème / Cream Earl Grey* — with vanilla.
- *London Fog* — an *Earl Grey latte*: strong Earl Grey with steamed milk and vanilla syrup. *Canadian in origin* (Vancouver, 1990s), despite the name; now a standard café drink.
- *Smoky Earl Grey* — on a *Lapsang Souchong* (正山小種) base rather than a plain China black.
- *Green, white and rooibos "Earl Grey"* — bergamot applied to a different base entirely; the rooibos version contains *no Camellia and no caffeine* and is a different substance sharing only the flavouring. See red-tea.typ for the rooibos / "red tea" naming clash.
- *Decaffeinated Earl Grey* — the caffeine removed from the base; the bergamot fraction unaffected.

*Culinary extension*, which is substantial and modern: Earl Grey *shortbread*, *chocolate* and *truffles*, *ice cream*, *panna cotta* and *cake*, together with tea-infused baking generally — a late-20th-century pastry convention in which the bergamot aromatic, rather than the tea, is the flavour being sought.

=== Tibb (Arabic-Islamic)

*No classical compound preparation*, for the reasons given above. The indigenous tea traditions of the Islamic world run otherwise: *Maghrebi mint tea* (green tea with spearmint and sugar — see mint.typ, green-tea.typ) and the Levantine, Egyptian, Turkish and Persian black-tea traditions. *Earl Grey enters these as a British colonial-era import*, familiar in Egypt and the Levant as an imported branded tea rather than as a local preparation.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_Unlike jasmine tea, which is climate-coherent — grown, processed and drunk in one subtropical-humid zone — *Earl Grey is a compound of two substances from widely separated climates, assembled and consumed in a third that grows neither*. That makes this section unusually interesting and requires treating three zones rather than one. See_ climates.typ _for the canonical frameworks applied._

*Warm-dry Mediterranean — the bergamot's zone, and it is extraordinarily narrow.* The overwhelming majority of the world's bergamot comes from a coastal strip of the province of *Reggio Calabria*, in the toe of Italy: a specific microclimate of mild wet winters, hot dry summers and sea-moderated nights. *Plantings elsewhere — Côte d'Ivoire, Brazil, Argentina, Turkey — yield oil of measurably different composition*, which is why the Calabrian origin is a commercial designation and not merely a boast. In Ayurvedic terms this is a *Pitta-aggravating* zone with the load carried in *grīṣma* (summer); in 中醫 terms the external excesses are *燥 + 熱* (dryness and heat) with Lung and Liver loaded; in Greco-Roman humoral terms, a choleric climate. *The traditional correctives are cooling and moisture-retaining* — and *the aromatic bitter-sour citrus peel is a native corrective of exactly this zone*, moving the Liver qi that a hot dry summer constrains. *Note what the Calabrians do not do with it, though:* bergamot is used there as *perfume* and as a small culinary flavouring, not drunk in quantity. *The fruit's own climate gave the world the oil and not the beverage.*

*Subtropical-humid and monsoonal — the tea base's zone.* Classically the Anhui and Fujian red-tea regions (祁門, 正山小種); in modern blends the Assam lowlands, the Sri Lankan and Nilgiri hills, and Kenya. Per jasmine-tea.typ and red-tea.typ: the Ayurvedic load is an extended *varṣā*-*śarad* Pitta-Kapha burden, the 中醫 reading is *濕熱* (damp-heat) with the Spleen loaded across most of the year (_Su Wen_ ch. 12, 異法方宜論). *Full oxidation is the warming answer within the tea family*, and it suits the *cooler and higher-altitude margins* of the tea zone — Anhui's winters, Darjeeling's elevation — better than the hot lowland summer, where the lighter, cooler-processed teas are the climatically apt ones.

*Cool-damp Atlantic — Britain, which grows neither plant and which invented and consumes the compound.* Mild wet winters, cool damp summers, little seasonal extreme. The Ayurvedic load is *Vāta-Kapha*; the 中醫 reading is external *寒濕* (cold-damp) with *Spleen-yang* loaded; the correctives per climates.typ are warming, long-cooked, salted and fat-bearing preparations. *And here the surprising result: the compound fits the climate that grows none of it.* A fully-oxidized *warm* tea base, carrying a *warm acrid aromatic*, taken *hot*, conventionally with *milk* or *sugar*, is a climatically coherent preparation for a cool-damp Vāta-Kapha zone — warming the middle, moving the qi that cold-damp stagnates, with a *snigdha* vehicle against the Vāta. *The two components are both warm-climate products and the compound is a cold-climate drink*, which is not a paradox but a consequence of how it was assembled: the British took a warming tea and made it warmer, which is what a cool-damp climate wants. *Cares, which are constitutive rather than optional in this reading:* taken *hot*, not iced; *near-boiling water* (unlike the cooler brewing green and scented teas need); *milk or sugar* where the drying edge wants buffering, which is the Tibb *muṣliḥ* logic arrived at independently; and *afternoon rather than evening*, the caffeine load being what it is. *This whole paragraph is this book's own reasoning, not a classical or documented claim* — the British did not design the blend on humoral grounds, and the coherence is retrospective.

*Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26).* Earl Grey is now drunk globally, and two transpositions are worth naming. *Iced and bottled Earl Grey in hot-humid climates* inverts every care listed above — the hot, warming, aromatic preparation served cold in a *濕熱* zone where the constitutional need is light, bitter-pungent and drying. It is not harmful, but it is off-key, and the aromatic-warming register contributes nothing the climate requires. *The London Fog is the sharper case:* a warm aromatic with steamed milk, sugar and vanilla is a *cool-damp-climate construction* — heavy, snigdha, warming — and serving it in a tropical or subtropical café is a clear *deśa-viruddha*, the Pitta-Kapha load of the climate meeting a preparation built to answer Vāta-Kapha. *In parallel, the _kāla-viruddha_ case:* hot, heavily-milked, sugared Earl Grey at the height of summer *in any climate* is a seasonal mismatch for the same reasons, and the iced form — however off-key in a damp-heat *deśa* — is at least the right *kāla*.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*The base carries the substantive pharmacology and is not restated here* — theaflavins and thearubigins as the dominant polyphenols, catechins largely depleted by full oxidation, caffeine, L-theanine. See red-tea.typ for the oxidation chemistry and green-tea.typ for the Camellia framework.

*Bergamot peel oil constituents.* The *volatile* fraction, which is what the flavouring is for:
- *Linalyl acetate* — roughly 30–45%, the principal character compound.
- *Limonene* — roughly 25–45%; the generic citrus terpene.
- *Linalool* — roughly 5–20%; *the compound with the best-characterized pharmacology in the oil*, and already discussed in this book for jasmine tea, where it does the same job. See jasmine-tea.typ.
- *γ-terpinene, β-pinene, nerol, neryl acetate, geraniol* — the remainder of the spectrum.
The *non-volatile oxygen-heterocyclic* fraction, which is what the safety discussion is about:
- *Bergapten (5-MOP)*, *bergamottin (5-geranyloxypsoralen)*, *citropten (5,7-dimethoxycoumarin)*.
*Proportions vary widely by origin, season and extraction*, and *FCF oil has the second group stripped out* while synthetic flavourings never contained it.

*How much actually reaches a cup, which is the question most writing on this subject skips.* The oil is applied at a *fraction of a percent by weight of leaf*; the furanocoumarins are the *non-volatile, poorly water-soluble* fraction and are *inefficiently extracted into an infusion*; and the aromatic experience of the tea comes overwhelmingly from the volatile terpenes. *Quantitative data on furanocoumarin content in brewed Earl Grey is sparse*, which is itself worth stating — the honest position is that the exposure is small and imprecisely characterized rather than that it is zero or that it is well-measured. *This is reasoned from the preparation and the chemistry, and flagged as such.*

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Linalool and linalyl acetate:* anxiolytic and mild sedative effects are reasonably well characterized, *predominantly in animal models and in human inhalation studies* rather than in ingestion trials. The convergence worth marking is that *this is the same compound class that carries jasmine tea's mood register* — two scented teas from unrelated traditions arriving at the same pharmacology. The Chinese *疏肝解鬱*, the Ayurvedic *manas-prasādana*, the Tibb *mufarriḥ* and the linalool literature all point at the same register; *that convergence is real, and the effect size at tea-strength doses is unestablished*.
- *CYP3A4 inhibition:* bergamottin and bergapten are furanocoumarin CYP3A4 inhibitors — the grapefruit mechanism. See angelica-root.typ for the fuller treatment of the mechanism in this book and tangerine-peel.typ for a milder instance. *Clinically modest-to-negligible at ordinary Earl Grey consumption*, for the four reasons given under Viruddha; the real-world case is heavy consumption of naturally-flavoured product with critical-dose substrates.
- *Phototoxicity:* a *topical* phenomenon (berloque dermatitis, phytophotodermatitis), not a consequence of drinking the tea. Route matters and is frequently conflated.
- *The single human toxicity report:* the 2002 *Lancet* correspondence at ~4 L/day, discussed under Viruddha. *n = 1, unreplicated, extreme dose.*

*What must not be imported into this entry, and it is the main honesty point.* There is a genuine clinical literature on *bergamot polyphenolic fraction (BPF)* — an extract of the *juice and albedo*, containing the flavonoids *brutieridin* and *melitidin*, which have structural affinity for HMG-CoA reductase and have shown lipid-lowering effects in small randomized trials. *This is not evidence about Earl Grey, and reviews and popular health writing cite it as though it were.* Three separations, any one of which would be sufficient: it is *a different part of the fruit* (juice and albedo, not peel oil); it is *a different chemical fraction* (non-volatile flavonoid glycosides, not volatile terpenes); and it is *a different dose by orders of magnitude* (gram-scale standardized extract, versus traces of oil in an infusion). *Drinking Earl Grey has not been shown to affect blood lipids, and the BPF trials do not bear on the question.* This is the same discipline this book applies in refusing to read single-herb data as evidence for a compound formula — see six-gentlemen-decoction.typ — pointed in the other direction.

*What has not been shown:* that Earl Grey differs from plain black tea in any measured health outcome; that the bergamot fraction at tea-strength doses has any demonstrated clinical effect, anxiolytic or otherwise; and that naturally-flavoured and synthetically-flavoured Earl Grey differ in any respect other than furanocoumarin content. *All three are open, and the first is the one most often assumed.*

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* as for the red-tea base — *3–5 cups/day* (~600–1000 mL) for healthy non-pregnant adults; well tolerated. *The limiting component is caffeine, not bergamot*: ≤400 mg/day for healthy adults and ≤200 mg/day in pregnancy per EFSA, which at 40–60 mg/cup puts the ceiling well above ordinary consumption. *Secondary consideration:* anyone on a critical-dose CYP3A4-metabolized medication who drinks naturally-flavoured Earl Grey heavily should raise it with a prescriber — and should know that FCF and synthetically-flavoured products remove the issue entirely. *Iron-deficient:* separate from iron-rich meals by 1–2 hours. *Citrus allergy:* avoid. *Reference figures requiring current verification, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Bergamot oil composition: standard essential-oil analytical literature on _Citrus bergamia_; the linalyl acetate / limonene / linalool proportions vary substantially by origin and season and the ranges given are indicative. Furanocoumarin content and FCF processing: IFRA and food-flavouring regulatory literature. The estimate that infusion exposure is small is reasoned from the application rate and the non-volatile fraction's water-insolubility and is flagged as reasoned, not measured; published quantitative data on brewed Earl Grey is sparse. Linalool anxiolytic pharmacology: predominantly animal-model and inhalation literature, as already noted in `substances/jasmine-tea.typ`. CYP3A4 furanocoumarin inhibition: see `substances/angelica-root.typ` and `substances/tangerine-peel.typ`. The "Earl Grey intoxication" report is a _Lancet_ correspondence (2002) describing a single patient at ~4 L/day — cited with its dose and its n, and not generalized. Bergamot polyphenolic fraction (BPF), brutieridin and melitidin, and the lipid trials: a real literature on a juice-and-albedo extract at gram doses, which does not bear on tea consumption; the refusal to import it is this book's standard. EFSA Scientific Opinion on the safety of caffeine for the dosage ceiling. All modern figures flagged for verification against current literature.]

